  {
    "episode_id": 95264,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95264.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "Bob, I'd be honest with you. I haven't watched a whole lot of it. The president and I have been actually doing a fair amount of travel this week. The president, unlike the Democrats, the president was in Wisconsin Monday. I was in Wisconsin yesterday, and we were carrying the message of all the progress we made in our first three years, all this president's leadership throughout this coronavirus pandemic. But from what I've seen, it does seem to be a convention that has confirmed everything that we've been saying all along, that Joe Biden and the Democratic Party have been overtaken by the radical left. And we've seen one speech after another that while when they're not attacking the president, we see people like Elizabeth Warren and Bernie Sanders articulating uh... the kind of left wing liberal policies that are far out of the mainstream of this country and uh... i'm just count the dates for when the republican national convention starts next week and and we can lay out of the contrast i mean in in joe biden the democratic party the radical left you've got you've got a group that wants uh... higher taxes uh... socialized medicine open borders abortion on demand And what's to cut funding the law enforcement at a time of rising violence in our streets and President Trump and our entire administration we've got a record of less taxes, free market solutions, standing up for life and liberty and standing without apology for law enforcement. So I'm excited about next week, but the overall tone and tenor of the Democrat National Convention just has confirmed to us the the liberal leftward term of the Democratic Party. And I just know that when it comes to November 3rd, we're going to win four more years. President Donald Trump of the White House.",
